# direnv config — auto-loaded on `cd` into this project if you have direnv.
#
# macOS Sequoia auto-hides anything literally named `.venv` inside
# iCloud-synced folders (~/Documents, ~/Desktop). Python 3.12+ silently
# skips hidden .pth files, which breaks editable installs. So uv's default
# of `.venv` is wrong here twice over — and renaming it to `venv` only
# addressed the hiding, not the syncing.
#
# A cloud-synced folder is not a safe place for a virtualenv AT ALL. Inside
# one, iCloud has repeatedly damaged this project's env in place: duplicate
# `name 2.py` conflict copies, two dist-info directories for one package
# (which made `importlib.metadata.version` return None), and on 2026-08-01 a
# `transformers` tree left with 226 of its 2347 `.py` files — which raised
# `KeyError: frozenset()` on import and took `memory_search` down entirely.
# See docs/incidents/2026-08-01-broken-optional-extra-killed-retrieval.md.
#
# So: inside a synced folder the venv goes OUTSIDE it, keyed by the checkout's
# directory name so sibling worktrees keep separate envs. Anywhere else the
# in-repo `venv` is fine and is left exactly as it was.
case "$PWD" in
  "$HOME/Documents"/* | "$HOME/Desktop"/*)
    export UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT="$HOME/.venvs/$(basename "$PWD")"
    ;;
  *)
    export UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=venv
    ;;
esac

# Without direnv, export the same value by hand before running uv, or activate
# the env directly. `uv` with neither set defaults to an in-repo `.venv`, which
# inside a synced folder is the case above.
#
# One consequence worth knowing: with the env outside the repo there is no
# in-repo `.venv`, and `[tool.pyright]` in pyproject.toml points at one, so
# pyright reports every import as missing. Symlink it — `.gitignore` matches
# `.venv` without a trailing slash so the symlink is ignored, and the synced
# folder then holds 0 bytes instead of the environment:
#
#     ln -s "$UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT" .venv

